#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Replace markdown syntax with HTML tags
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex regex(R"((\*([^\*]+)\*)|(\*\*([^\*]+)\*\*)|(\#([^\n]+))");
    std::regex_replace(html, html, regex, "<em>$1</em>");
    std::regex_replace(html, html, regex, "<strong>$1</strong>");
    std::regex_replace(html, html, regex, "<h1>$1</h1>");
    return html;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to replace markdown syntax with HTML tags. The regular expression matches text enclosed in double asterisks (`**`) and replaces it with a `<strong>` tag, text enclosed in asterisks (`*`) and replaces it with an `<em>` tag, and text enclosed in hashtags (`#`) and replaces it with an `<h1>` tag.

Note that this function assumes a basic understanding of markdown syntax and may not handle all edge cases. For a more robust solution, you may want to consider using a dedicated markdown-to-HTML library, such as the MarkdownC library or the Pandoc library.

